course: dictionary
lesson: 5610
title: Ligaments: the straps that hold bones together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A ligament is a strong band in your body. It is a bit stretchy, and it joins one bone to another bone at a joint, like your knee or ankle. «The knee is a hinge joint held together by strong straps called ligaments.» Those straps are the ligaments, and they keep the bones in place.
explain: «Ligaments join bone to bone, while tendons join muscle to bone.» Look at the two words that are being compared: bone to bone and muscle to bone. The sentence tells you the difference. Ligaments are the ones that connect two bones.
explain: «A rolled ankle stretches or tears the ligaments, the tough straps that hold the joint together.» The sentence explains the word for you. The ligaments are tough straps, and they hold the joint together. When they are stretched or torn, the ankle swells and hurts.
explain: Check a new word by putting your meaning in the sentence. Try it: «A ligament is a tough strap that holds a joint together.» Does it still make sense? If it does, you understand the word.
fact: A ligament joins one bone to another bone.
fact: Tendons join muscle to bone, and ligaments join bone to bone.
fact: «Sprain» means an injury where ligaments are stretched or torn a little.
fact: Ligaments hold joints together and stop them bending the wrong way.
fact: Warming up before sport helps to protect your ligaments.
exercise: «A rolled ankle stretches or tears the ligaments, the tough straps that hold the joint together.» What are ligaments? | *Tough straps that hold a joint together | Muscles that move the foot | Bones at the top of the leg | Blood vessels in the skin
exercise: «Ligaments join bone to bone, while tendons join muscle to bone.» Which one joins bone to bone? | Skin | Tendons | *Ligaments | Muscles
exercise: «A sprain is a joint injury: the ligaments get stretched or torn a little, like when you twist your ankle in football.» What is a sprain? | *An injury where ligaments are stretched or torn a little | A strong muscle used for kicking | A soft cushion of cartilage | A bone that has broken in two
test: «The knee is a hinge joint held together by strong straps called ligaments.» Which body parts hold the knee together? | Muscles | *Ligaments | Tendons only | Skin
test: «Sore knees in a growth spurt need rest and a check, and a torn knee ligament means surgery.» What does torn mean here? | Long and thin | Stretched but still whole | *Ripped or split apart | Warm and swollen
exam: «Ligaments hold joints together and stop them bending the wrong way.» What is the job of ligaments? | *To hold joints together and stop them bending the wrong way | To soften the skin around a joint | To make muscles move | To carry blood to the bones
